## Summary

The compromised npm package `axios@1.14.1` (now yanked from the registry) could be pulled in transitively when installing GitNexus, through the `@ladybugdb/core` dependency.

`@ladybugdb/core`'s `install.js` runs a bare `npm install` internally (line 67) when building from source:

```js
childProcess.execSync("npm install", {
  cwd: path.join(__dirname, "lbug-source", "tools", "nodejs_api"),
  stdio: "inherit",
});
```

This internal `npm install` resolves dependencies from the public registry at install time. While `axios@1.14.1` was live on npm (before it was yanked), this could resolve to the compromised version, which contained a `setup.js` dropper script.

## Attack Chain

```
npm exec gitnexus@latest (or npm install gitnexus)
  → installs @ladybugdb/core
    → install.js runs bare "npm install" internally
      → npm resolves axios@1.14.1 from registry (compromised)
        → axios postinstall executes setup.js dropper
```

