Replace the ~200-line monolithic `resolveCallTarget` with a 15-line dispatcher that routes to `resolveMemberCall`, `resolveStaticCall`, or `resolveFreeCall`. The D0–D4 fuzzy widening paths are removed; legitimate resolution strategies (module-alias disambiguation, file-based fallback, overload disambiguation) are extracted into focused helpers.

### Dispatcher (`resolveCallTarget`)

```typescript
const resolveCallTarget = (...): ResolveResult | null => {
  const tiered = ctx.resolve(call.calledName, currentFile);
  if (!tiered) return null;

  if (call.callForm === 'free')
    return resolveFreeCall(...);
  if (call.callForm === 'constructor')
    return resolveStaticCall(...) ?? singleCandidate(tiered, ...);
  if (call.receiverTypeName) {
    // skip owner-scoped path when overload disambiguation is needed
    return resolveMemberCall(...) ?? resolveMemberCallByFile(...) ?? singleCandidate(tiered, ...);
  }
  return resolveModuleAliasedCall(...) ?? singleCandidate(tiered, ...);
};
```

### Extracted helpers

- **`resolveModuleAliasedCall`** — Python/Ruby `import mod; mod.Symbol()` patterns via `moduleAliasMap`, with constructor-form retry and widen-to-global fallback
- **`resolveMemberCallByFile`** — file-based + ownerId fallback for member calls where owner-scoped resolution fails (Rust trait dispatch, cross-file overloads), includes overload disambiguation via `tryOverloadDisambiguation` / `matchCandidatesByArgTypes`
- **`singleCandidate`** — tail fallback returning the sole survivor from the tiered pool after callable + arity filtering

### Test updates

- Renamed `resolveCallTarget D0 skip conditions (SM-11)` → `resolveCallTarget thin dispatcher (SM-19)` with updated expectations (module-alias homonym case now returns null — genuine ambiguity without fuzzy path)
- Updated section header comments and doc comments referencing deleted D0–D4 internals

### Known regressions (2 tests)

Two tests show slightly more permissive behavior from the `singleCandidate` tail fallback vs the old receiver-type-aware null-route: Rust qualified-syntax MRO and call-processor no-heritageMap. Both are edge cases where the old D1–D4 zero-match guard was stricter. Addressable in a follow-up with a receiver-type null-route guard.

