## Summary

`gitnexus impact` (CLI and MCP) crashes with a SIGSEGV (exit 139) on any symbol whose impacted callers participate in execution flows (Processes) or modules (Communities). Via the MCP transport this surfaces as a "Connection closed" error because the server process dies silently.

Confirmed on **gitnexus 1.4.1**.

## Reproduction

Any sufficiently-connected symbol triggers it. In a real-world repo (~187k nodes, 380k edges):

```bash
$ gitnexus impact --repo wp-content --direction upstream --depth 2 getSearchQuery
$ echo $?
139
```

The crash is silent — no stack trace, no error message.

## Root Cause

In `dist/mcp/local/local-backend.js` around line 1257, the `impact` handler runs three enrichment queries concurrently via `Promise.all`:

```javascript
const [processRows, moduleRows, directModuleRows] = await Promise.all([
    executeQuery(repo.id, /* STEP_IN_PROCESS query */),
    executeQuery(repo.id, /* MEMBER_OF query */),
    d1Ids ? executeQuery(repo.id, /* MEMBER_OF d1 query */) : Promise.resolve([]),
]);
```

The connection pool (`dist/mcp/core/kuzu-adapter.js`) gives each `executeQuery` its own `kuzu.Connection`, so this looks safe. But the underlying `kuzu.Database` object appears to not be safe for concurrent queries from multiple connections in the same process — running these three queries simultaneously crashes the Node process at the C++ binding layer.

## Why it only crashes on certain symbols

The enrichment block is gated by `if (impacted.length > 0)`. For symbols where the depth traversal returns no results (e.g. classes with no upstream callers, methods picked from files unrelated to processes), the enrichment block is skipped and no crash occurs. For symbols whose impacted callers participate in many processes, the parallel enrichment queries race and crash.

## Minimal repro

```javascript
import { initKuzu, executeQuery, executeParameterized } from 'gitnexus/dist/mcp/core/kuzu-adapter.js';

await initKuzu('repo-id', '/path/to/.gitnexus/kuzu');

// Get a symbol with rich connections
const targets = await executeParameterized('repo-id',
  `MATCH (n) WHERE n.name = $name RETURN n.id AS id LIMIT 1`,
  { name: 'someMethodWithProcessConnections' });
const symId = targets[0].id;

// Get its callers
const r2 = await executeQuery('repo-id',
  `MATCH (caller)-[r:CodeRelation]->(n) WHERE n.id = '${symId}' AND r.type IN ['CALLS'] RETURN caller.id AS id`);
const allIds = r2.map(r => `'${r.id}'`).join(', ');

// This crashes:
await Promise.all([
  executeQuery('repo-id', `MATCH (s)-[r:CodeRelation {type: 'STEP_IN_PROCESS'}]->(p:Process) WHERE s.id IN [${allIds}] RETURN p.heuristicLabel, COUNT(s.id), MIN(r.step), p.stepCount LIMIT 20`),
  executeQuery('repo-id', `MATCH (s)-[:CodeRelation {type: 'MEMBER_OF'}]->(c:Community) WHERE s.id IN [${allIds}] RETURN c.heuristicLabel, COUNT(s.id) LIMIT 20`),
  executeQuery('repo-id', `MATCH (s)-[:CodeRelation {type: 'MEMBER_OF'}]->(c:Community) WHERE s.id IN [${allIds}] RETURN DISTINCT c.heuristicLabel`),
]);
// SIGSEGV
```

Running the same three queries serially with `await` succeeds in <300ms.

## Suggested Fix

Replace the `Promise.all` block with serial `await` calls. Loses some parallelism but kuzu queries are fast enough that it doesn't matter in practice — we measured ~250ms for all three serially on a 187k-node graph.

```javascript
const processRows = await executeQuery(repo.id, /* STEP_IN_PROCESS */).catch(() => []);
const moduleRows = await executeQuery(repo.id, /* MEMBER_OF */).catch(() => []);
const directModuleRows = d1Ids
  ? await executeQuery(repo.id, /* MEMBER_OF d1 */).catch(() => [])
  : [];
```

## Broader Concern

If kuzu's Database object is genuinely not safe for concurrent queries from the same process, every other `Promise.all([executeQuery(...), executeQuery(...)])` pattern in the codebase is a latent crash. A grep for that pattern would be worth doing.

## Environment

- gitnexus 1.4.1
- Node.js v24.9.0
- macOS Darwin 24.6.0
- kuzu DB size: 586MB (186,731 nodes, 380,013 edges)

## Summary

`ScopeTreeInvariantError: non-module-requires-parent` aborts `scopeResolution` for any C# file whose `compilation_unit` and single top-level `namespace_declaration` have **identical** tree-sitter ranges. The error is silently swallowed by `extractParsedFile` (`gitnexus/src/core/ingestion/scope-extractor-bridge.ts:43-54`) and surfaces as `scope extraction failed for ...` warnings under `gitnexus analyze -v`. The file is then dropped from the registry-primary path; downstream resolver loses every cross-file edge that touches it.

```
scope extraction failed for X.cs: Scope 'scope:X.cs#L:C-L:C:Namespace' has kind 'Namespace' but no parent. Only 'Module' scopes may be root-level.
```

The triggering shape is **not BOM-specific** and **not size-specific** — it is purely a property of the file's start/end content.

## Reproduction

Direct probe through `extractParsedFile(csharpProvider, src, ...)`. Four byte shapes, all containing only a single top-level `namespace_declaration`:

| # | Shape | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | UTF-8 BOM + CRLF + `namespace X { class Y {} }` (no trailing newline) | **FAILED** — `Scope 'scope:Test.cs#2:0-7:1:Namespace' has kind 'Namespace' but no parent.` |
| 2 | `namespace X\n{\n    class Y {}\n}` (LF, no trailing newline) | **FAILED** — `Scope 'scope:Test.cs#1:0-4:1:Namespace' has kind 'Namespace' but no parent.` |
| 3 | `namespace X\n{\n    class Y {}\n}\n` (LF, with trailing newline) | ok |
| 4 | UTF-8 BOM + CRLF + `namespace X { ... }` + CRLF (with trailing newline) | ok |

Cases (1) and (2) are equivalent — both end exactly at the closing `}` of the namespace, so tree-sitter-c-sharp 0.23.1 reports identical ranges for `compilation_unit` and `namespace_declaration`. Cases (3) and (4) extend `compilation_unit` by one row past `namespace_declaration`, so strict containment holds.

## Real-world impact

Hit on the [PersistentWindows](https://github.com/kangyu-california/PersistentWindows) WinForms repo — three auto-generated `*.Designer.cs` files (`HotKeyWindow.Designer.cs`, `LaunchProcess.Designer.cs`, `DbKeySelect.Designer.cs`) all begin with `<BOM><CRLF>namespace ... { ... }` and end with `}<no-trailing-newline>`. Last-16-byte hexdump confirms: `... 7D 0D 0A 7D` (closing brace, CRLF, closing brace) — final byte is the namespace's closing `}` with nothing after it.

Cutover comparison on the same repo:

|  | nodes | edges | clusters | flows | freeze err | scope-extract err |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| pre-#1082 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | many | (aborted earlier) |
| #1082 (current) | 1113 | 2987 | 39 | 97 | 0 | **3** (these Designer.cs files) |

The 3 dropped files are not catastrophic for this repo (they are partial-class designer halves), but on a codebase whose primary entry points happen to live in such files, every CALL/IMPORTS edge crossing into them disappears.

## Root cause

`pass1BuildScopes` (`gitnexus/src/core/ingestion/scope-extractor.ts:311-357`) assigns each scope's parent by stack-popping until the stack-top range strictly contains the candidate's range:

```ts
while (stack.length > 0 && !rangeStrictlyContains(stack[stack.length - 1]!.range, cand.range)) {
  stack.pop();
}
const parent = stack.length > 0 ? stack[stack.length - 1]!.id : null;
```

`rangeStrictlyContains` (`gitnexus/src/core/ingestion/scope-extractor.ts:910-926`) explicitly returns **false** for identical ranges:

```ts
function rangeStrictlyContains(outer: Range, inner: Range): boolean {
  if (
    outer.startLine === inner.startLine &&
    outer.startCol === inner.startCol &&
    outer.endLine === inner.endLine &&
    outer.endCol === inner.endCol
  ) {
    return false;
  }
  ...
}
```

Sequence for case (2):

1. Module candidate from `(compilation_unit) @scope.module` — range `0:0-3:1`. Pushed.
2. Namespace candidate from `(namespace_declaration) @scope.namespace` — range `0:0-3:1`. While-loop sees coincident ranges → pops Module. Stack empty. `parent = null`. Pushed.
3. `buildScopeTree` (`gitnexus-shared/src/scope-resolution/scope-tree.ts:99-105`) sees Namespace with `parent === null` and throws `non-module-requires-parent`.

The strict-containment posture is sound between siblings (Module-and-Namespace at the same range is the only natural coincidence); it just doesn't model the universal-outer relationship between `compilation_unit` and a same-range top-level scope.

## Suggested fixes

Two minimally invasive options. Either one passes the empirical 4-case repro above; both also pass the existing 207-test C# resolver suite.

**(a)** Tighten the `pass1BuildScopes` while-loop to allow a same-range Module to keep parenthood:

```ts
while (stack.length > 0) {
  const top = stack[stack.length - 1]!;
  if (rangeStrictlyContains(top.range, cand.range)) break;
  // Module is the universal outer — keep it as parent when ranges coincide.
  if (top.kind === 'Module' && rangesEqual(top.range, cand.range) && cand.kind !== 'Module') break;
  stack.pop();
}
```

Plus a sort tie-breaker so Module always sorts before a same-range non-Module:

```ts
if (a.kind !== b.kind) {
  if (a.kind === 'Module') return -1;
  if (b.kind === 'Module') return 1;
}
return 0;
```

**(b)** Loosen `rangeStrictlyContains` itself to permit equal-range parenting only when kinds differ:

```ts
function canParentScope(outer: Range, inner: Range, outerKind: ScopeKind, innerKind: ScopeKind): boolean {
  if (rangeStrictlyContains(outer, inner)) return true;
  if (outerKind === 'Module' && innerKind !== 'Module' && rangesEqual(outer, inner)) return true;
  return false;
}
```

Used at the parent-finder call site only; `rangeStrictlyContains` keeps its current strict semantics for sibling/lookup contexts.

Option **(a)** is strictly local to `pass1BuildScopes`; option **(b)** carves out a named helper for the contract. Happy to submit either as a follow-up PR with a regression fixture (`csharp-namespace-as-root-no-trailing-newline`) plus a unit test on `pass1BuildScopes` directly. Let me know your preference.

## Cross-language note

`rangeStrictlyContains` and `pass1BuildScopes` are language-agnostic. Other languages whose query emits `(compilation_unit) @scope.module` plus a single same-range top-level scope (Python's `module` matches its single top-level `class_definition` or `function_definition` only when the file contains exactly that one declaration with no leading/trailing trivia, etc.) could in principle hit the same shape. Did not chase those — flagging in case the contract layer turns out to be the better fix tier.

