## Out of scope / observations

- I did not touch package versions, dependencies, or lockfiles.
- The `gitnexus-claude-plugin/hooks/gitnexus-hook.js` and `gitnexus/hooks/claude/gitnexus-hook.cjs` files share substantial logic and drift apart. Not in this PR's scope, but a future refactor to a single source-of-truth (with a small build step that emits the `.cjs` variant) would prevent the next "fix one, miss the other" issue.
- For Bug 1 I went with the `git rev-parse` approach rather than the `~/.gitnexus/registry.json` lookup since the existing hook already shells out to `git` for HEAD checks — this keeps it consistent and avoids a new file-format dependency in the hook. Happy to switch approach if you'd prefer the registry path.

## Summary

When `gitnexus analyze` is run from inside a [git worktree](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree), the resulting project is registered under the **worktree directory's basename** (e.g. `awesome-morse-3440a3`) instead of the canonical repo name (e.g. `app`). This produces three observable issues:

1. **Duplicate registry entry** in `~/.gitnexus/registry.json` — the same codebase appears twice, once for the main checkout and once per worktree.
2. **`AGENTS.md` rewrite with the wrong name** — the auto-generated block between `<\!-- gitnexus:start -->` markers references the worktree slug in MCP resource paths (`gitnexus://repo/awesome-morse-3440a3/...`). If the contributor doesn't notice the diff and ships the rewrite, every future Claude Code session in the repo gets the wrong MCP paths.
3. **MCP queries against the worktree-named project don't match the canonical project's index**, so `gitnexus_impact` / `gitnexus_context` etc. return data for whichever copy is fresher.

## Root cause (verified against `gitnexus@1.3.11`)

Two code paths combine. Line numbers from the published npm `dist/`:

`dist/storage/git.js`:
```js
export const getGitRoot = (fromPath) => {
    const raw = execSync('git rev-parse --show-toplevel', { cwd: fromPath })
        .toString().trim();
    return path.resolve(raw);
};
```

`git rev-parse --show-toplevel` returns the **worktree's** root, not the canonical repo's root. From a worktree at `/path/to/repo/.git/worktrees/foo/` (linked-worktree style: `/path/to/repo/wt-foo/`), it returns the worktree path.

`dist/cli/analyze.js:268`:
```js
const projectName = path.basename(repoPath);
```

`repoPath` resolves to the worktree path via the chain above (when `analyze` is invoked without an explicit `[path]` argument). `path.basename` of a worktree's path is the worktree's directory name.

## Reproduction

```bash
# In any repo
git worktree add ./wt-feature feature-branch
cd ./wt-feature
npx gitnexus analyze
npx gitnexus list   # observe a NEW project entry whose name is "wt-feature"
cat AGENTS.md       # observe `gitnexus://repo/wt-feature/...` instead of canonical project name
```

## Suggested fix

Use `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` instead of (or in addition to) `--show-toplevel`. The "common dir" is the `.git` directory shared across worktrees — it's the canonical anchor. Its parent is the canonical repo root regardless of which worktree the caller is in:

```js
// dist/storage/git.js
export const getCanonicalRepoRoot = (fromPath) => {
    try {
        const commonDir = execSync(
            'git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir',
            { cwd: fromPath },
        ).toString().trim();
        // Common dir is `<repo>/.git` for both main checkout and worktrees.
        // Parent is the canonical repo root.
        return path.dirname(path.resolve(commonDir));
    } catch {
        return null;
    }
};
```

Then in `analyze.js`, derive `projectName` from the canonical root rather than the worktree's `--show-toplevel`. (Or expose a `--project-name` flag as a backstop for users with non-standard layouts.)

## Workaround for users today

Pass the canonical repo path explicitly to the optional `[path]` arg:

```bash
# from a worktree
npx gitnexus analyze "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir | xargs dirname)"
```

I've shipped a project-local wrapper script for our team that does this resolution: <https://github.com/altworth-markets/app/pull/277> (see `scripts/gitnexus.sh`).

## Environment

- `gitnexus@1.3.11` (npm, global install)
- Node 22.21.1
- macOS 25.4.0 (Darwin); reproducible on any platform with `git worktree`
- Trigger: any [Claude Code worktree](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/cli) (it shells `git worktree add` under `.claude/worktrees/<slug>/`), but the bug applies to any `git worktree`-based workflow.

## Why this matters in practice

[Claude Code](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/cli) creates a new worktree per task by default. Teams using it heavily will have many worktrees per repo, and every one of them silently registers a duplicate project + rewrites `AGENTS.md` on the first analyze. The first symptom is usually a confused contributor whose committed `AGENTS.md` diff includes a project rename they didn't make.

## Description

On Windows, `gitnexus setup` writes an incorrect `command` path to the Claude Code MCP config (`~/.claude.json`). The written path points to the POSIX shell script (`.../npm/gitnexus`) instead of the Windows-compatible `.cmd` wrapper (`.../npm/gitnexus.cmd`). This causes the MCP health check to fail with `spawn ENOENT` / `spawn EINVAL` because Node.js `child_process.spawn()` on Windows cannot execute a shell script directly.

## Root Cause

The `resolveGitnexusBin()` function uses `where gitnexus` on Windows (line 26-33 in `setup.js`). On Windows, `where` returns multiple lines:

```
C:\...\npm\gitnexus      <- POSIX shell script (first line)
C:\...\npm\gitnexus.cmd  <- Windows batch wrapper (second line)
```

The code takes only the first line via `.split('\n')[0]`, which picks the shell script -- the wrong file for Windows `spawn()`.

```js
// setup.js:24-38
function resolveGitnexusBin() {
    const cmd = process.platform === 'win32' ? 'where' : 'which';
    const resolved = execFileSync(cmd, ['gitnexus'], ...)
        .split('\n')[0]       // picks the shell script
        .trim();
    return resolved || null;
}
```

When `bin` is returned, `getMcpEntry()` writes it directly as the `command`:
```js
if (bin) {
    return { command: bin, args: ['mcp'] };
}
```

The fallback path for when `bin` is `null` (lines 56-60) already handles Windows correctly but never executes because `resolveGitnexusBin()` always finds the shell script on a system with a global install.

On Windows, `resolveGitnexusBin()` picks the first line from `where gitnexus`, which is the POSIX shell script. Node.js `child_process.spawn()` cannot execute it directly, causing `spawn ENOENT` when the MCP health check runs.

**Fix:**

- Scan all lines from `where` output and prefer the entry matching `/\.(cmd|bat)$/i`
- Falls back to first line if no wrapper found (safety for unusual installs)

```typescript
// Before: always took first line (shell script on Windows)
.split('\n')[0].trim();

// After: on Windows, find the .cmd/.bat wrapper
const lines = output.split('\n').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
if (isWin) {
  const cmdLine = lines.find((l) => /\.(cmd|bat)$/i.test(l));
  return cmdLine || lines[0] || null;
}
```

**Tests added:**
- Verifies `.cmd` is selected when `where` returns both shell script and `.cmd`
- Verifies fallback to first line when no wrapper exists
- Verifies CRLF line endings (as produced by Windows `where`) are handled correctly
- Verifies `.bat` wrapper selection
- Verifies case-insensitive matching (uppercase `.CMD`)

**Spawn compatibility note:** The `.cmd` wrapper is the standard Windows npm convention for CLI binaries. Claude Code's MCP launcher is expected to handle `.cmd` files, as this is how npm exposes all global CLI tools on Windows. The previous behavior (`ENOENT` from attempting to spawn the extensionless POSIX script) was strictly worse — the fix changes a guaranteed failure into the correct conventional path.

When the worker pool's idle timeout fires and splits a job, `activeWorkers--` was decremented **before** `await replaceWorker()`. During that async gap, other workers finishing could call `maybeDone()`, see `activeWorkers === 0`, and resolve the pool with incomplete results — causing silent exit code 0 with no index written.

## Changes

- **Move `activeWorkers--` to after the `await`** — eliminates the window where `maybeDone()` can observe zero active workers while split jobs are queued but not yet picked up
- **Guard `replaceWorker()` failure path** — wraps `await replaceWorker()` in try/catch so that if worker thread creation fails, `activeWorkers` is decremented and `fail()` is called rather than leaving the pool hanging indefinitely
- **Add regression test** — verifies the sole-active-worker timeout/split scenario completes rather than resolving early

```typescript
// Before (race condition during await):
activeWorkers--;
const shouldContinue = requeueAfterTimeout(workerIndex, job, lastProgress);
if (!shouldContinue) return;
await replaceWorker(workerIndex);  // ← gap: activeWorkers=0, jobs queued
runWorker(workerIndex);

// After:
const shouldContinue = requeueAfterTimeout(workerIndex, job, lastProgress);
if (!shouldContinue) { activeWorkers--; return; }
try {
  await replaceWorker(workerIndex);
} catch (err) {
  activeWorkers--;
  void fail(err instanceof Error ? err : new Error(`Worker replacement failed: ${err}`));
  return;
}
activeWorkers--;          // ← no gap: synchronous with runWorker below
runWorker(workerIndex);
```

## Summary

A `const`-bound exported object literal with method shorthand (`export const fooService = { async barMethod() { ... } }`) produces graph nodes with **zero relationships** — neither the singleton-to-method edges nor the incoming call-site edges from consumers are linked. This breaks `gitnexus_impact` for any chokepoint exported in this style: it always returns `risk: LOW, direct: 0` even when production has many live callers, silently disarming the pre-edit safety gate that AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md mandate.

## Versions

- `gitnexus@1.6.4-rc.69` (installed via `ONNXRUNTIME_NODE_INSTALL=skip npm install -g gitnexus@1.6.4-rc.69`)
- Node `v22.22.0`
- TypeScript source files (commonjs/esm-mixed monorepo, Express 5 backend)

## Repro

A real production file in our codebase, `backend/src/auth/credential-service.ts`:

\`\`\`typescript
export const credentialService = {
  async verifyPassword(email: string, plaintext: string): Promise<CredentialVerifyResult> {
    // ...
  },
  async verifyPin(email: string, pin: string, deviceFingerprint: string): Promise<CredentialVerifyResult> {
    // ...
  },
};
\`\`\`

Consumer at `backend/src/auth/login-handler.ts:331`:

\`\`\`typescript
import { credentialService } from './credential-service';
// ...
verifyResult = await credentialService.verifyPassword(email, request.password);
\`\`\`

Same pattern repeats for `refreshFamily.rotateRefreshFamily` (`backend/src/auth/refresh-family.ts` exports `refreshFamily` const → `backend/src/api/auth/refresh.routes.ts:183` calls `refreshFamily.rotateRefreshFamily(tx, claims, ip_address)`).

## Run

\`\`\`bash
npx gitnexus clean --force
npx gitnexus analyze --embeddings
\`\`\`

Then via the MCP server (or equivalent CLI query):

\`\`\`json
{ "tool": "context", "args": { "name": "credentialService" } }
{ "tool": "context", "args": { "name": "verifyPassword", "file_path": "backend/src/auth/credential-service.ts" } }
{ "tool": "impact",  "args": { "target": "verifyPassword", "direction": "upstream", "file_path": "backend/src/auth/credential-service.ts", "maxDepth": 3 } }
\`\`\`

## Actual

\`\`\`json
// context({name: "credentialService"})
{
  "status": "found",
  "symbol": { "uid": "Const:backend/src/auth/credential-service.ts:credentialService", ... },
  "incoming": {},
  "outgoing": {},   // <-- expected HAS_PROPERTY → verifyPassword, verifyPin
  "processes": []
}

// context({name: "verifyPassword", file_path: "backend/src/auth/credential-service.ts"})
{
  "status": "found",
  "symbol": { "uid": "Method:backend/src/auth/credential-service.ts:verifyPassword#2", ... },
  "incoming": {},   // <-- expected CALLS from login-handler.ts:loginHandler
  "outgoing": {},
  "processes": []
}

// impact(verifyPassword, upstream)
{
  "impactedCount": 0,
  "risk": "LOW",
  "summary": { "direct": 0, "processes_affected": 0, "modules_affected": 0 },
  "byDepth": {}
}
\`\`\`

\`grep -rnE "credentialService\\.verifyPassword" backend/src/\` confirms the call site exists in source.

## Expected

- \`context({name: "credentialService"})\` outgoing should contain HAS_PROPERTY (or equivalent) edges to \`verifyPassword\` and \`verifyPin\` Method nodes.
- \`context({name: "verifyPassword"})\` incoming should contain a CALLS edge from the function in \`login-handler.ts\` that dispatches \`credentialService.verifyPassword(...)\`.
- \`impact(verifyPassword, upstream)\` should report at least one direct caller and surface MEDIUM/HIGH risk for an auth chokepoint.

## Impact

Auth/audit chokepoints exported as singleton object-literals are exactly the surface where the pre-edit safety gate is most needed. False-LOW results encourage agents to edit these files without enumerating call sites, which is the failure mode `impact_analysis` is designed to prevent. Workaround in our project (codified in `bmad-dev-story/assurement-canon-supplement.md`): cross-verify every LOW result on a singleton-pattern symbol with grep before HALT/PROCEED — but this defeats the purpose of having the graph.

## Notes

- Same pattern repeats for `class FooService { method() {} }; export const fooService = new FooService();` (the second polluting form) — both verifyPin (line 130) on the same file and `rotateRefreshFamily` on the namespace-style export both miss edges.
- ACCESSES edges (per the README's mention of field-access tracking) also appear absent for `singleton.method` reads — consistent with the parser bottoming out at the `.` operator on these export shapes.
- Filed against \`@abhigyanpatwari/gitnexus\` v1.6.4-rc.69 (the WAL-fixed RC line); no regression tested against earlier versions because 1.6.3 is unusable for us due to the WAL corruption issue (#1314 — fixed by commit 0273402).

